!!! Byte access to text.
!!!
!!! nil.std.text slices and measures by byte, and until text.byte_at and
!!! text.from_byte existed there was no way to read a byte as a number or
!!! write one as text. The conversion that looked like it would do it,
!!! convert.char_to_i32, takes a whole Unicode scalar and aborts the process
!!! on a partial one, so a byte inside a multi-byte character was unreachable
!!! and so was percent-encoding anything outside ASCII.
!!!
!!! What this page pins down is that these two are about bytes and not about
!!! characters, which is the whole reason they were added.

book matrix.byte-access

read nil.std.core
read nil.std.text.string_utils

main returns number begin paragraph
    !!! An ASCII byte reads as its code and writes back as itself.
    say nil.std.text::byte_at with s being "A", index being 0
    say nil.std.text::from_byte with code being 65

    !!! Out of range answers rather than raising, so a scan can discover the
    !!! end by reading past it.
    say nil.std.text::byte_at with s being "A", index being 1
    say nil.std.text::byte_at with s being "A", index being 0 minus 1

    !!! Two bytes built one at a time make one character, because joining text
    !!! joins bytes. This is the case convert.i32_to_char cannot express: it
    !!! would give the two bytes that encode U+00C3 for a request for 195.
    value one-character is (nil.std.text::from_byte with code being 195)
        plus (nil.std.text::from_byte with code being 169)
    say nil.std.text::length with s being one-character
    say nil.std.text::byte_at with s being one-character, index being 0
    say nil.std.text::byte_at with s being one-character, index being 1

    !!! The same character written as a scalar is the same two bytes, which is
    !!! what proves the byte route and the scalar route agree.
    value as-scalar is nil.std.core::Convert.i32_to_char with code being 233
    say nil.std.text::length with s being as-scalar
    say (as-scalar equals one-character) becomes text

    !!! Every byte value is reachable, including zero and the high half that
    !!! is not valid UTF-8 on its own.
    say nil.std.text::length with s being (nil.std.text::from_byte with code being 0)
    say nil.std.text::byte_at with s being (nil.std.text::from_byte with code being 255), index being 0

    !!! Outside the byte range there is nothing to give.
    say nil.std.text::length with s being (nil.std.text::from_byte with code being 256)
    say nil.std.text::length with s being (nil.std.text::from_byte with code being 0 minus 1)
    give 0
end paragraph
